ct `(_c1<<8)|_c0` / `(_c0<<8)|_c1` index arithmetic so they work as lvalues (`*BUCKET_A(..) += 1`, `--BUCKET_BSTAR(..)`).\n- Both lookup tables were extracted from the C source programmatically and diffed against it (an early hand-reflow of `lg_table` had 257 entries; now byte-exact).\n- Comma-operator/side-effect ordering (`*a-- = *b, *b-- = *a`), post-decrement guards (`if(limit-- == 0)`), sticky function-scope `x`/`r` variables, arithmetic `>>1` on signed ints, and short-circuit assignment inside `sssort`'s buffer condition are all preserved.\n\n**Verification beyond type-checking**: I built the original C (renamed to `c_divsufsort`/`c_divbwt`, `-fPIC -O2 -DNDEBUG`) into a static lib and linked it against the Rust module, then compared results over 4000 randomized cases (n up to 5000; alphabets of 2/3/5/17/256; periodic inputs to stress equal-run paths) plus edge cases (n = 0,1,2,3, NULL `T`, negative `n`): suffix arrays, BWT strings, primary index, `num_indexes` and the whole secondary `indexes` array all matched exactly — **0 mismatches**.\n\n**One discrepancy to flag**: the task brief gave `divbwt` as 5 parameters, but this repo's `c_src/src/dictBuilder/divsufsort.{c,h}` declares 7 — `divbwt(const unsigned char *T, unsigned char *U, int *A, int n, unsigned char *num_indexes, int *indexes, int openMP)`. I matched the actual C source (7 params) so the exported ABI and `construct_BWT_indexes` reachability match the C build; `divsufsort` is 4 params as specified. Note also that only `divsufsort` is actually called inside the project (`zdict.c:506`); `divbwt` is exported but unused."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":787,"cache_read_input_tokens":153865,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":787,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01F38TsXijsdVc1Zx88TEFch","session_id":"91081e3