{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_x5ux4ptn6de53iwfuxsz6wob4efbxenmufci37za4yiatzmydela","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The entire zstd 1.5.7 C library in `c_src/` (39 `.c` files, ~58k lines, plus the code-carrying headers) is translated to Rust in `src/`, building as a `cdylib`.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| gate | result |\n|---|---|\n| `cargo build --release` | clean — 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n| `nm -D` symbol diff vs the C `.so` | **615 / 615** — 0 missing, 0 extra |\n| differential execution vs the C `.so` | **11,749 transcript lines, byte-identical** |\n\n## How it's organised\n\n61 Rust files / 78k lines, mirroring the C tree (`src/common/`, `compress/`, `decompress/`, `dictbuilder/`, `deprecated/`, `legacy/`). The 7,843-line `zstd_compress.c` became six part files `include!`d into one module, so they share private helpers exactly as they share one C translation unit.\n\nI reproduced the CMake configuration exactly, including the non-obvious bits:\n- `XXH_NAMESPACE=ZSTD_` — the macro-renamed linker symbols are `ZSTD_XXH32`, `ZSTD_XXH64_digest`, … not `XXH*`.\n- `ZSTD_TRACE == 1` (gcc weak symbols) — so `ZSTD_CCtx`/`ZSTD_DCtx` *must* keep a `traceCtx` field even though the hooks are NULL. My struct-layout harness caught this: without it `sizeof(ZSTD_CCtx)` was 5272 instead of 5280.\n- `ZSTD_ARCH_X86_SSE2` **is** on (x86-64 gcc always defines `__SSE2__`), so `zstd_lazy`'s row match-finder uses real SSE2 intrinsics; `__AVX2__` is off, so the scalar `convertSequences_noRepcodes`/`ZSTD_get1BlockSummary` are the live ones.\n- libc `malloc`/`qsort_r` rather than Rust equivalents — sort tie-breaking is observable in dictionary output.\n\n## Three things worth flagging\n\n**The differential harness is the real evidence.** It `dlopen`s either `.so` and digests every output: all levels −5…22 over 5 corpora, 27 advanced parameters, 6 MB inputs with/without LDM and with small `windowLog` (extDict paths), streaming, dictionaries (`ZDICT` + cover + fastcover), sequence/block/skippable-frame APIs, static contexts, and 1,350 corruption/truncation cases.\n\n**No reliance on UB.** The same transcript came out of three builds — `opt-level=2`, `opt-level=0`, and `overflow-checks=on` — all identical to C. The overflow-checked build completing *without a panic* is the strong claim: every place the C relies on wrapping is written with explicit `wrapping_*`. That audit is what found `ZSTD_highbit32(0)` (reached from `FSE_optimalTableLog_internal` with `srcSize == 1`), which I made explicitly wrapping.\n\n**One genuine translation bug found and fixed.** The first differential run differed on exactly one line: `ZSTDv02_decompress` on a modern frame returned −4 instead of −10. `zstd_v02.c` includes `error_private.h` *before* its own error enum, so that private enum is `#ifndef`-ed away and the compiled code uses the modern `ZSTD_ErrorCode` values — a subtlety the v03 translation got right and v02 initially got wrong.\n\nTwo caveats on test scope, both documented in `README.md`: feeding random bytes to the `legacy/` decoders segfaults the *reference C build* (they predate hardening), so only well-formed-input behaviour is compared there — and for the same reason the corruption fuzz leaves the 4 magic bytes intact, since flipping them re-routes data into those legacy decoders.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified — I did accidentally create some empty scratch directories inside it early on via a stale shell cwd, and removed them; it's back to its original 93 files / 9 directories."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":479,"cache_read_input_tokens":816282,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":479,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91081e33-7aae-4a44-80fe-383759459deb","uuid":"f2e56622-f8ac-4e52-b138-49d331b1d280","timestamp":"2026-08-22T23:48:10.027Z"}
